course: dictionary
lesson: 4641
title: Learning a long word from its clues
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words are long and hard to say, like hippopotamus. Do not panic. Look at what the words around it tell you. «Whales are mammals whose ancestors walked on land about 50 million years ago; their closest living land relative is the hippopotamus.» The sentence talks about relatives and land animals, so the hippopotamus is a real animal that lives on land and is related to whales.
explain: Now read this: «At first she thinks the splashing creature is a walrus or hippopotamus, but, being so small, she sees it is only a mouse.» A splashing creature that a person might mistake for a walrus is probably big and lives near water. The word «small» in the same sentence is the surprise. Use the clues to test your idea.
explain: Some words come with a short form. Hippopotamus is often shortened to hippo, which is easier to write and say. Check the meaning by swapping the word into the sentence: if «hippo» still makes sense there, you have the right word.
fact: A hippopotamus is a very large, heavy African animal with thick grey skin, a huge mouth and short legs.
fact: Hippos spend most of the day in rivers and lakes.
fact: The short form of hippopotamus is hippo.
fact: In science, the eyes, ears and nostrils of a hippo are on top of its head, so it can see and breathe while almost under water.
fact: Hippos look slow and friendly, but they are among the most dangerous animals in Africa.
exercise: What is a hippopotamus? | A fast horse that runs across dry plains | A small grey bird that lives high in trees | A fish with thick, spiky scales | *A very large, heavy African animal that lives in rivers and lakes
exercise: What is the short form of hippopotamus? | hip | hippy | potty | *hippo
exercise: Hippos look slow and friendly, but what are they according to safety facts? | Quiet pets that live in homes | *Among the most dangerous animals in Africa | Very small and shy | Able to fly over the river
test: In science lessons, why are a hippo's eyes, ears and nostrils on top of its head? | So it can hear music better | *So it can see and breathe while almost under water | So it can keep the sun out of its eyes | So it looks like a crown
test: In the phrase «as big as a hippo», what does the hippo show? | Something that can fly | Something very tiny | Something dark green and thin | *Something very large
exam: Which of these is NOT a correct plural of hippopotamus? | *hippopotamuse | hippopotami and hippopotamuses are both correct, so pick the one that is not | hippopotamuses | hippopotami
